  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge and 850 mb thermal ridge will continue to \nstrengthen through Tuesday, bringing continued warming to the area. \nForecast highs Monday are in the upper 80s to mid 90s, and then low \nto upper 90s Tuesday. We'll likely see an increase in high clouds \nTuesday as upper level flow turns more out of the south direction \nand is able to tap into some high level moisture as a trough moves \nin along the West Coast. With the nocturnal low level southerly jet \nkicking in every night, and gradual increase in cloud cover, lows \nlook to be mild/warm (in the upper 60s to low 70s). \n\nWeather pattern gets interesting mid to late next week. Model and \nensemble guidance is split on whether the trough along the West \nCoast becomes progressive and pushes east across the \nCentral/Northern plains which will in turn send the first strong \ncold front of the meteorological Fall season through the region \nsometime Thursday or Friday (which is what the 12Z GFS and most of \nthe 12Z GEFS members depict) or whether the trough develops into a \ncut-off low across the Four Corners, resulting in the cold front \nstaying to the north (which is what the 00Z ECMWF and 50% of the 00Z \nEC Ensemble members depict). Regardless of whether the cold front \ncomes down or not, all the model and ensemble guidance agrees that \nrain chances will increase Tuesday night through Friday as \nincreasing moisture and lift comes in due to the nearby trough \n(latest NBM guidance has rain chances peaking at medium chances (40-\n60%) late Wednesday night). Ensemble guidance right now shows the \naverage total of around 0.25-0.75 inches, with a heavier scenario \n(low (10%) chance) bringing a widespread 2+ inches. It will also get \ncooler, due to the cloud cover/rain, and then potentially much \ncooler if the cold front moves through. Due to the high uncertainty, \nthe latest forecast depicts one last day of hot temperatures \nWednesday (highs in the low to mid 90s) before cooling to the mid to \nupper 80s Thursday, mid to upper 70s Friday, and low to mid 70s next \nSaturday.",
